---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} image="/images/case-studies/lofoten-coast-og.jpg">
  <Header />

  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />

  <main class="cs-page">
    <CaseStudyHero
      crumbLabel="Guides"
      crumbHref="/payment-solutions/case-studies/#guides"
      title={guide.title}
      titleId="article-title"
      variant="article"
    >
      Guide · Mexico · <time datetime={guide.published}>{publishedLabel}</time> · {guide.readingMinutes} min read
    </CaseStudyHero>

    <article class="cs-article" aria-labelledby="article-title">
      <p class="cs-dek">
        In Mexico, you can buy something online and pay for it later, in cash,
        at a convenience store. That is one of at least six ways to pay. For an
        online store, each one has a different cost, a different risk and a
        different moment when the money arrives. Many stores handle all of them
        with one company: Mercado Pago. This guide explains how it works, what
        it really costs, and when it makes sense to use it.
      </p>

      <dl class="cs-facts-line">
        <div><dt>For</dt><dd>{guide.audience}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Market</dt><dd>Mexico (MXN)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Level</dt><dd>From zero</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <aside class="cs-note" aria-label="About this guide">
        <strong>About this guide</strong>
        <p>
          This guide is for educational purposes only and is not financial,
          legal or business advice. The figures come from public sources:
          Mercado Pago's price pages and developer documentation, Mercado
          Libre's annual report, Banco de México and national surveys. They
          were checked in November 2026. Fees, interest rates and rules change
          often, and errors are possible, so check the current numbers in your
          own account before making a decision. The people and the store in the
          examples are illustrations; the fees and rates are real. I am not
          affiliated with Mercado Pago or Mercado Libre.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <section class="cs-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
        <h2 id="summary-title">Summary</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Mexican shoppers pay online with debit cards, credit cards, interest-free months, cash at convenience stores, bank transfers and app credit. A good checkout needs most of them.</li>
          <li>Mercado Pago gives a store all of these through one connection, without a direct contract with a bank. It also gives shoppers an account with a balance, saved cards and credit.</li>
          <li>The fee for a single payment depends on when the store wants its money: from 2.95% to 3.49% plus IVA.</li>
          <li>Twelve interest-free months on a bank card cost the store about MXN 190 on a MXN 1,000 sale. Twelve months on Mercado Pago's own credit line cost the store nothing extra, but cost the shopper about MXN 592.</li>
          <li>The main risks are price, money held during disputes, dependence on one provider and the extra step in the shopper's journey.</li>
          <li>Integration ranges from a payment link (no code) to a fully custom checkout. The most important part is the webhook, not the payment page.</li>
          <li>In 2025, 60% of Mercado Pago's revenue in Mexico came from credit, not from payments.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <nav class="cs-toc" aria-labelledby="toc-title">
        <h2 id="toc-title">In this guide</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#mexico">How Mexico pays online</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what">What Mercado Pago is and where it came from</a></li>
          <li><a href="#terms">Twelve terms, with examples</a></li>
          <li><a href="#when">When a store should use Mercado Pago</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-risks">Why stores connect to it, and the risks</a></li>
          <li><a href="#costs">What it really costs: four scenarios</a></li>
          <li><a href="#business">Where Mercado Pago makes its money</a></li>
          <li><a href="#integration">How to connect a store: the options</a></li>
          <li><a href="#conclusions">Conclusions</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">What people ask about Mercado Pago</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- 1. Mexico -->
      <section id="mexico" aria-labelledby="mexico-title">
        <h2 id="mexico-title">How Mexico pays online</h2>
        <p>
          To follow the costs and the decisions in this guide, we will use one
          purchase from start to finish. Sofía lives in Guadalajara. She wants
          a ceramic lamp from Luz de Barro, a small online store run by Daniel.
          The lamp costs MXN 1,000.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before Sofía reaches the checkout, some context. In person, Mexico
          still pays mostly in cash: in the 2024 national financial inclusion
          survey, 85% of adults said cash is how they usually pay for anything
          under MXN 500. At the same time, Mexico has a bank transfer system,
          SPEI, that moves money between banks in seconds. In 2025, it carried
          more than 7 billion transfers. Cards sit in the middle: about 11
          billion card payments were made in Mexico in 2025, and only 28% of
          them were with credit cards.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is a checkout with more options than in many other
          markets. These are the ways Sofía might want to pay:
        </p>

        <figure class="cs-compare">
          <figcaption>How shoppers pay online in Mexico</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Method</th>
                  <th scope="col">How it works</th>
                  <th scope="col">What the store should know</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  methods.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{row.method}</th>
                      <td>{row.how}</td>
                      <td>{row.note}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <p>
          A store that only accepts cards loses everyone who wants to pay in
          cash or by transfer. A store that sells expensive items without
          monthly payments loses many card customers too. In Mexico, a good
          checkout needs most of these methods, and each one behaves
          differently after the shopper clicks "Pay". I explain why so much of
          Mexico still pays in cash, despite its fast transfer system, in
          <a href="/journal/2026-11-03-mexico-built-the-rails-so-why-does-it-still-pay-in-cash/">Mexico built the rails. So why does it still pay in cash?</a>
        </p>

        <p class="cs-bridge">Accepting all of these methods used to mean several contracts with several companies. This is the problem Mercado Pago solves.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. What -->
      <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-title">
        <h2 id="what-title">What Mercado Pago is and where it came from</h2>
        <p>
          Mercado Pago is the payments and financial services company of
          Mercado Libre, the largest online marketplace in Latin America. It
          was not created for other stores. It was created to make strangers
          trust each other.
        </p>

        <figure class="cs-facts" aria-labelledby="facts-title">
          <figcaption id="facts-title">Mercado Pago: key facts</figcaption>
          <dl>
            {
              facts.map((fact) => (
                <div>
                  <dt>{fact.label}</dt>
                  <dd>{fact.value}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </figure>

        <p>
          In the early years of Mercado Libre, buying from a stranger had a
          simple problem. Picture someone selling a used camera. If you pay
          first, the seller might never ship it. If the seller ships first, you
          might never pay. Mercado Pago solved this by standing in the middle.
        </p>

        <ol class="cs-timeline">
          {
            timeline.map((item) => (
              <li>
                <span class="cs-timeline-year">{item.year}</span>
                <p>{item.text}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>

        <p>
          Today, Mercado Pago has two sides, and this is what makes it
          different from most payment companies.
        </p>
        <ul class="cs-plain-list">
          <li>
            <strong>For stores</strong>, it collects payments: online, with
            small card readers called Point, with QR codes and with payment
            links that can be sent by WhatsApp.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>For people</strong>, it is an account. Sofía can keep a
            balance in it, save her cards, get a debit or credit card, and
            borrow.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Most payment companies only work for the store. Mercado Pago works
          for both sides of the payment at once. If Sofía already has a Mercado
          Pago account, paying Daniel does not mean typing sixteen digits. It
          means logging in and choosing what she has already saved. When both
          Sofía and Daniel use Mercado Pago and she pays from her balance, the
          money never leaves the company: one record goes down and another
          goes up.
        </p>

        <p class="cs-bridge">Before we look at costs and risks, here are the terms that appear in every conversation about Mercado Pago.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. Terms -->
      <section id="terms" aria-labelledby="terms-title">
        <h2 id="terms-title">Twelve terms, with examples</h2>
        <dl class="cs-terms">
          {
            terms.map((item) => (
              <div>
                <dt>{item.term}</dt>
                <dd>
                  <p>{item.meaning}</p>
                  <p class="cs-term-example"><span>Example</span>{item.example}</p>
                </dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>

        <p class="cs-bridge">With these terms in place, the first business question is simple: should a store in Mexico use Mercado Pago at all?</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. When -->
      <section id="when" aria-labelledby="when-title">
        <h2 id="when-title">When a store should use Mercado Pago</h2>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Strong fit</span> Mercado Pago is a strong choice when:</h3>
          <ul class="cs-plain-list">
            {fit.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Compare first</span> It is worth comparing other options when:</h3>
          <ul class="cs-plain-list">
            {compare.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p>
          The last point is my own view, not something Mercado Pago says. For
          a small or medium store, Mercado Pago can be the whole checkout. For
          a large retailer, it is usually one option among several, there
          because so many shoppers already have a balance, saved cards or
          credit inside it.
        </p>

        <p class="cs-bridge">For a store like Daniel's, the reasons to connect are clear. So are the risks.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Why and risks -->
      <section id="why-risks" aria-labelledby="why-risks-title">
        <h2 id="why-risks-title">Why stores connect to it, and the risks</h2>
        <p>
          Normally, to accept cards, a store signs a contract with a bank, an
          <em>afiliación</em>. It means paperwork, sometimes a minimum amount of
          sales and sometimes a monthly fee. Mercado Pago removes that step. It
          already has the contracts with the card networks and the banks, and
          Daniel's store becomes one of many small merchants under its
          umbrella. Daniel opens an account, connects his store and starts
          selling. The money from his sales arrives in his Mercado Pago
          account, and from there he can send it to his bank.
        </p>

        <h3>Three reasons to connect</h3>
        <ol class="cs-steps">
          <li>
            <h3>One connection, every method</h3>
            <p>
              Cards (including Carnet, Mexico's local card brand), interest-free
              months, cash at OXXO and other stores, SPEI transfers, the
              shopper's Mercado Pago balance and Mercado Pago's own credit
              line.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Security handled by the provider</h3>
            <p>
              Mercado Pago runs the fraud checks and the extra identity step
              called 3D Secure, the moment when Sofía's bank may ask her to
              approve the payment in its app. It also has a team that reviews
              payments that look risky.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>A faster checkout for existing users</h3>
            <p>
              Shoppers with an account pay with what they have already saved.
              Shoppers without an account can still pay as guests.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Four risks to plan for</h3>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Risk 1</span> The price</h3>
          <p>
            Mercado Pago is easy to set up, but it is not the cheapest option.
            A large store with its own bank contract can usually negotiate a
            lower rate. The next section shows the real numbers.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Risk 2</span> Money that gets held</h3>
          <p>
            Imagine Sofía tells her bank: "I did not make this purchase." That
            is a chargeback, and the bank takes the money back while it checks.
            Mercado Pago then freezes the same amount in Daniel's account until
            he proves he delivered the lamp. Mercado Pago says a hold like this
            can last from a few days to several weeks, and in serious cases the
            account itself can be suspended.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Risk 3</span> Depending on one company</h3>
          <p>
            If all of Daniel's payments go through one provider, any problem
            with that account stops his sales. That is why larger stores often
            connect two or three payment providers, and send each payment to
            the one most likely to approve it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Risk 4</span> The shopper's journey</h3>
          <p>
            In the simplest version, Sofía leaves Daniel's website, pays on a
            Mercado Pago page and comes back. Every extra screen is a moment
            when a shopper can give up. Shoppers who already have an account,
            however, may finish faster there. The balance depends on how many
            of your customers already use Mercado Pago.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="cs-bridge">The first risk deserves numbers. Here is what Daniel actually keeps from Sofía's MXN 1,000 lamp.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 6. Costs -->
      <section id="costs" aria-labelledby="costs-title">
        <h2 id="costs-title">What it really costs: four scenarios</h2>
        <p>
          All prices below are Mercado Pago's public prices for online sales in
          Mexico, checked in November 2026. A 16% tax, IVA, is added to every
          fee. All amounts are in Mexican pesos, and your own account may show
          slightly different rates.
        </p>

        <div class="cs-point" id="scenario-1">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Scenario 1</span> Sofía pays at once, and Daniel chooses when he gets the money</h3>
          <p>
            With Mercado Pago, the price of a single payment depends on how
            fast the store wants its money.
          </p>
          <figure class="cs-compare">
            <figcaption>A MXN 1,000 sale paid in one go</figcaption>
            <div class="cs-table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Daniel receives the money</th>
                    <th scope="col">Fee</th>
                    <th scope="col">Fee + IVA (MXN)</th>
                    <th scope="col">Daniel keeps (MXN)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {
                    releaseCosts.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <th scope="row">{row.when}</th>
                        <td>{row.fee}</td>
                        <td>{row.total}</td>
                        <td>{row.keeps}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))
                  }
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <p>
            Waiting a month instead of being paid immediately saves Daniel
            about MXN 6 on every MXN 1,000. That sounds small. But imagine his
            store sells MXN 200,000 a month online. Waiting 30 days saves him
            about MXN 1,250 a month, or about MXN 15,000 a year (an
            illustration). The question is real for every store: is having the
            money today worth that much?
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">Now Sofía wants to spread the payment over a year, using her bank credit card.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="scenario-2">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Scenario 2</span> Sofía pays in interest-free months on a bank card</h3>
          <p>
            Sofía chooses 12 <em>meses sin intereses</em>. She pays MXN 83.33 a
            month for a year: MXN 1,000 in total, with no interest. But
            Mercado Pago pays Daniel without waiting for Sofía's monthly
            payments, and that service has a price. With interest-free months,
            the store pays it, as an extra fee on top of the normal one.
          </p>
          <figure class="cs-compare">
            <figcaption>The same MXN 1,000 sale, with interest-free months (amounts in MXN)</figcaption>
            <div class="cs-table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Sofía pays in</th>
                    <th scope="col">Extra fee</th>
                    <th scope="col">Normal fee</th>
                    <th scope="col">Extra fee amount</th>
                    <th scope="col">IVA</th>
                    <th scope="col">Total cost</th>
                    <th scope="col">Daniel keeps</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {
                    msiCosts.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <th scope="row">{row.term}</th>
                        <td>{row.extraRate}</td>
                        <td>{row.normal}</td>
                        <td>{row.extra}</td>
                        <td>{row.iva}</td>
                        <td>{row.total}</td>
                        <td>{row.keeps}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))
                  }
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <p>
            Twelve interest-free months cost Daniel about MXN 190 on a MXN
            1,000 sale: almost one peso in every five. If his profit on the
            lamp is 10%, an assumption, offering 12 months wipes it out
            completely. That is why many stores offer long interest-free
            periods only on expensive items, set a minimum amount, or include
            the cost in the price.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two notes on these rates. They are the surcharges Mercado Pago
            publishes for interest-free months on its payment link page, and
            the checkout dashboard shows its own rate for each term. In the
            dashboard, the store also decides whether it absorbs the cost or
            passes it to the shopper.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">But what if Sofía has no credit card at all?</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="scenario-3">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Scenario 3</span> Sofía uses Mercado Pago's credit line instead</h3>
          <p>
            Sofía has no credit card, but her Mercado Pago app offers her a
            credit line. Mercado Pago calls this <em>meses sin tarjeta</em>,
            months without a card.
          </p>
          <p>
            For Daniel, this is simple. He pays only his normal fee and keeps
            MXN 959.52, the same as if Sofía had paid at once. Mercado Pago pays
            him the full amount, and Sofía pays Mercado Pago back over time.
          </p>
          <p>
            This time, however, Sofía pays interest. Mercado Pago publishes an
            average interest rate of 82.7% a year for this credit line, before
            tax. Divided by 12, that is about 6.9% a month: every month, Sofía
            pays about MXN 7 for every MXN 100 she still owes, plus 16% IVA on
            that interest. Over 12 equal payments, the table looks like this:
          </p>
          <figure class="cs-compare">
            <figcaption>12 monthly payments of MXN 132.66 at the average rate (amounts in MXN)</figcaption>
            <div class="cs-table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Month</th>
                    <th scope="col">Owes at start</th>
                    <th scope="col">Interest (6.89%)</th>
                    <th scope="col">IVA on interest</th>
                    <th scope="col">Pays off the lamp</th>
                    <th scope="col">Owes at end</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {
                    amortisation.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <th scope="row">{row.month}</th>
                        <td>{row.start}</td>
                        <td>{row.interest}</td>
                        <td>{row.iva}</td>
                        <td>{row.principal}</td>
                        <td>{row.end}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))
                  }
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row"><strong>Total</strong></th>
                    <td></td>
                    <td><strong>510.24</strong></td>
                    <td><strong>81.64</strong></td>
                    <td><strong>1,000.00</strong></td>
                    <td></td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <p>
            In total, Sofía pays MXN 1,591.87 for a MXN 1,000 lamp: MXN 510.24
            of interest and MXN 81.64 of tax on that interest. The payment
            never changes, but its content does. In the first month, only MXN
            52.71 of her MXN 132.66 pays off the lamp; by the last month,
            almost all of it does.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three things to keep in mind. First, 82.7% is an average: some
            people get lower rates and some higher ones, and the app shows the
            exact amount before the shopper confirms. Second, the calculation
            assumes 12 equal payments, made on time. Third, this price is
            similar to other digital lenders in Mexico, but much higher than
            the average bank credit card:
          </p>
          <figure class="cs-compare">
            <figcaption>Total annual cost (CAT, before tax) of consumer credit in Mexico</figcaption>
            <div class="cs-table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Product</th>
                    <th scope="col">CAT</th>
                    <th scope="col">Date</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {
                    creditContext.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <th scope="row">{row.product}</th>
                        <td>{row.cat}</td>
                        <td>{row.date}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))
                  }
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <p>
            Higher prices for digital credit partly reflect who borrows: many
            users have no credit history and would not get a bank card. Even
            so, the difference matters for anyone deciding how to pay.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">Put the three ways of paying side by side, and the pattern becomes clear.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="scenario-4">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Scenario 4</span> The same lamp, three ways</h3>
          <figure class="cs-compare">
            <figcaption>One MXN 1,000 lamp, three ways to pay (amounts in MXN)</figcaption>
            <div class="cs-table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">How Sofía pays</th>
                    <th scope="col">Daniel keeps</th>
                    <th scope="col">Sofía pays in total</th>
                    <th scope="col">Who pays for the wait</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {
                    threeWays.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <th scope="row">{row.how}</th>
                        <td>{row.keeps}</td>
                        <td>{row.pays}</td>
                        <td>{row.who}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))
                  }
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <p class="cs-takeaway">
            The same lamp, sold in 12 months, can cost the store MXN 190 or
            cost the shopper almost MXN 600. Someone always pays for the wait,
            and Mercado Pago earns in every column.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="cs-bridge">That last observation leads to a bigger question: where does Mercado Pago actually make its money?</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 7. Business model -->
      <section id="business" aria-labelledby="business-title">
        <h2 id="business-title">Where Mercado Pago makes its money</h2>
        <p>
          Mercado Libre's annual report for 2025 shows the company's revenue in
          five lines. The fintech business, Mercado Pago, brought in US$12.6
          billion, 44% of the total, and grew 46% in the year.
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-compare">
          <figcaption>Mercado Libre revenue by line, 2025 (US$ millions)</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Line</th>
                  <th scope="col">What it includes</th>
                  <th scope="col">Revenue</th>
                  <th scope="col">Share</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  revenue2025.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{row.line}</th>
                      <td>{row.what}</td>
                      <td>{row.amount}</td>
                      <td>{row.share}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><strong>Total</strong></th>
                  <td></td>
                  <td><strong>28,893</strong></td>
                  <td><strong>100%</strong></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <h3>How much comes from checkout?</h3>
        <p>
          The report does not say. Payment fees are split across two places,
          and neither is shown separately:
        </p>
        <ul class="cs-plain-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Payments on the Mercado Libre marketplace</strong> count as
            commerce revenue: the report says the marketplace's final value
            fees include the use of the payment solution.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Payments at other stores</strong> (online checkout, card
            readers and QR codes) are part of "financial services and income",
            together with interest on cash and investments, installments and
            insurance.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          What the report does show is volume: Mercado Pago processed US$188.1
          billion in payments for merchants in 2025, on and off the
          marketplace. How much of that it kept as revenue is not disclosed.
        </p>

        <h3>In Mexico, credit is already the larger business</h3>
        <figure class="cs-compare">
          <figcaption>Mercado Pago revenue in Mexico, 2025 (US$ millions)</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Line</th>
                  <th scope="col">Revenue</th>
                  <th scope="col">Share</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  mexicoFintech.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{row.line}</th>
                      <td>{row.amount}</td>
                      <td>{row.share}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><strong>Total</strong></th>
                  <td><strong>2,290</strong></td>
                  <td><strong>100%</strong></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p>
          In Mexico, Mercado Pago already earns more from lending than from
          payments. Credit also has a cost. Across the company, the provision
          for doubtful accounts, the money set aside for loans that may not be
          repaid, was US$3,091 million in 2025, compared with US$5,858 million
          of credit revenue. About 53 cents of every dollar earned from credit
          was set aside for expected losses.
        </p>

        <h3>Profit is reported by country, not by product</h3>
        <p>
          Mercado Libre does not report the profit of checkout, credit or the
          marketplace separately. Management judges each country by its
          "direct contribution": revenue minus the country's direct costs,
          before head-office costs. The report explains that this view
          considers all business lines in a country as a whole.
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-compare">
          <figcaption>Direct contribution by country, 2025 (US$ millions)</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Country</th>
                  <th scope="col">Revenue</th>
                  <th scope="col">Direct contribution</th>
                  <th scope="col">Margin</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  contribution.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{row.country}</th>
                      <td>{row.revenue}</td>
                      <td>{row.contribution}</td>
                      <td>{row.margin}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><strong>Total</strong></th>
                  <td><strong>28,893</strong></td>
                  <td><strong>5,903</strong></td>
                  <td><strong>20.4%</strong></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p>
          After head-office costs, income from operations was US$3.2 billion
          in 2025, an 11.1% margin.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-takeaway">
          My reading: Mercado Pago does not need checkout to be very
          profitable on its own. Checkout brings shoppers and merchants into
          the system. Once inside, they keep balances, borrow and buy on the
          marketplace. Payments bring people in; credit is where much of the
          money is made.
        </p>

        <p class="cs-bridge">For a store that decides to use Mercado Pago, the next question is practical: how much work is the integration?</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 8. Integration -->
      <section id="integration" aria-labelledby="integration-title">
        <h2 id="integration-title">How to connect a store: the options</h2>
        <p>
          There are four ways to connect a store to Mercado Pago. They go from
          no code at all to building everything yourself. The more you build,
          the more control you have over the experience, and the more work it
          takes.
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-compare">
          <figcaption>Four ways to connect a store to Mercado Pago</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Option</th>
                  <th scope="col">Where the shopper pays</th>
                  <th scope="col">Effort</th>
                  <th scope="col">Control</th>
                  <th scope="col">Best for</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  options.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
                      <td>{row.where}</td>
                      <td>{row.effort}</td>
                      <td>{row.control}</td>
                      <td>{row.bestFor}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 1</span> Payment link or plugin</h3>
          <p>
            Daniel can create a payment link in his account and send it by
            WhatsApp. If his store runs on Shopify, WooCommerce, Tiendanube or
            VTEX, he installs Mercado Pago's plugin and enters his account
            credentials. The checkout looks the way Mercado Pago designed it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 2</span> Checkout Pro</h3>
          <p>
            For a store with its own website and a developer. Sofía goes to a
            Mercado Pago payment page and comes back. Mercado Pago runs the
            payment page; the developer connects four building blocks, which
            matter for every option.
          </p>
          <ol class="cs-steps">
            {
              buildingBlocks.map((block) => (
                <li>
                  <h3>{block.title}</h3>
                  <p>{block.text}</p>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
          <figure class="cs-code">
            <figcaption>The order Daniel's server sends to Mercado Pago</figcaption>
            <pre><code>{codePreference}</code></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>
            Two details matter. The <code>external_reference</code> is the
            store's own order number, so every notification can be matched to
            the right order. And the <code>notification_url</code> is where
            Mercado Pago sends the webhook. Never ship because a shopper
            reached the "thank you" page; wait for the webhook and check the
            status.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 3</span> Checkout Bricks</h3>
          <p>
            Ready-made components, such as a card form, a list of payment
            methods or a payment status screen, placed on the store's own
            pages. Sofía never leaves Daniel's site. It is more work than
            Checkout Pro, but much less than building everything.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 4</span> Checkout API</h3>
          <p>
            Daniel's team builds every screen. When Sofía types her card
            number, Mercado Pago's code in her browser turns it into a token,
            so the real number never reaches Daniel's server. That keeps the
            store out of most of the strict card-security requirements. Cash
            methods work differently: through the Checkout API, the store can
            offer OXXO, Paycash, Banamex and BBVA Bancomer, and the payment
            stays pending, with an expiry date, until Sofía pays.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Five decisions that matter more than the code</h3>
        <p>For product managers and business teams, these decisions shape the result more than the choice of integration.</p>
        <ol class="cs-steps">
          {
            decisions.map((decision) => (
              <li>
                <h3>{decision.title}</h3>
                <p>{decision.text}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>

        <p class="cs-bridge">With the costs, the risks and the effort on the table, we can return to the original question.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 9. Conclusions -->
      <section id="conclusions" aria-labelledby="conclusions-title">
        <h2 id="conclusions-title">Conclusions</h2>
        <p>
          Mercado Pago is not just a "Pay" button. It is one account that
          works on both sides of a payment. For Daniel, it is a cash register
          that takes almost every way Mexicans pay online. For Sofía, it is
          where her balance, her cards and her credit are already saved.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a small or medium store in Mexico, it can be the whole checkout:
          fast to set up, with every major payment method, at a price that
          depends on how quickly the store wants its money. For a large store,
          it is usually one option among several, and the risks of holds,
          dependence and price deserve a plan.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every way of paying has a price, and someone always pays it. With a
          single payment, the store pays a fee, and the faster it wants its
          money, the more it pays. With interest-free months, the store pays
          for the wait. With Mercado Pago's credit line, the shopper pays for
          the wait.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-takeaway">
          Next time you see "pay in 12 months" at a Mexican checkout, ask one
          question: who is paying for the wait, the store or the shopper?
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">These are the questions that merchants and shoppers ask most often.</p>
      </section>

